/-!
# Tensor products of fields over a separably closed field

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 00I7 (lemma-separably-closed-irreducible),
specialized to field extensions (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-field-tensor-irreducible).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

open scoped TensorProduct
open AlgebraicGeometry

namespace IsSepClosed

/-- Over a separably closed field, the tensor product of two field extensions has a unique
minimal prime, and its affine spectrum is irreducible and connected.
Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 00I7 (lemma-separably-closed-irreducible)
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-field-tensor-irreducible).

Proof:
1. A perfect closure of `k` is algebraically closed: an algebraic element has a power that
is separable over `k`, hence lies in `k` by separable closedness
[def-kun-separably-closed]. Purely inseparable base change induces homeomorphisms on the
spectra of `K`, `L`, and `K ⊗[k] L` [lem-pc-homeo-pure-insep], preserving minimal primes.
Thus it suffices to work over that algebraically closed field.
2. The two base-changed factors each have one prime and their reductions are fields.
The kernel of tensoring the reduction maps is generated by nilpotent elements, so this
replacement preserves the spectrum. The tensor product of the resulting domains is reduced
[lem-weil-pbc-perfect-reduced-tensor].
3. A tensor product of nonzero vector spaces is nonzero. A nonzero zero divisor would already
occur in the tensor product of two finitely generated subalgebras
[lem-weil-pbc-tensor-finite-type-limit]. Thus reduce to finite type domains over the
algebraically closed field.
4. For these domains `R`, `S`, the map `R → R ⊗ S` is flat since `S` is a free vector space
[def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map]. It is finitely presented by a base-changed finite presentation
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-noetherian-finite-presentation]. At every maximal ideal of `R`,
the residue field is the base field [lem-weil-pbc-closed-point-residue-field-base], so the
fibre is the spectrum of `S`, which is irreducible. Maximal ideals are dense
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-field-prime-intersection-maximal]. The flat-fibre criterion
[lem-weil-pbc-flat-fibres-irreducible] now makes the total spectrum irreducible.
5. Reducedness [lem-weil-pbc-perfect-reduced-tensor], nontriviality, and irreducibility make
this tensor product a domain. Step 3 gives the same for the unrestricted reduced factors;
Steps 2 and 1 transport the unique minimal prime back to `K ⊗[k] L`. Its spectrum is the
closure of that prime, hence irreducible [def-weil-irreducible-components], and therefore
connected [def-weil-connected-scheme]. -/
theorem field_tensor_unique_minimalPrime
    (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsSepClosed k]
    (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra k K]
    (L : Type w) [Field L] [Algebra k L] :
    (∃! p : Ideal (K ⊗[k] L), p ∈ minimalPrimes (K ⊗[k] L)) ∧
      IrreducibleSpace (Spec (CommRingCat.of (K ⊗[k] L))) ∧
      ConnectedSpace (Spec (CommRingCat.of (K ⊗[k] L))) := by
  sorry

end IsSepClosed
